WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.540
&gt;&gt; Hi. I'm Asha Thomas.

00:00:06.541 --> 00:00:09.155
I represent Advaith Foundation.

00:00:09.156 --> 00:00:12.160
We are an NGO in
the space of education,

00:00:12.161 --> 00:00:14.950
especially K-12, focused on K-12.

00:00:14.951 --> 00:00:21.275
We started out looking at
the underserved children.

00:00:21.276 --> 00:00:23.950
We said we'll give them
access to schooling,

00:00:23.951 --> 00:00:25.650
and we'll take care of
their after school,

00:00:25.651 --> 00:00:27.820
because for them to be
successful in school,

00:00:27.821 --> 00:00:29.230
they need a lot of
support out of school.

00:00:29.231 --> 00:00:32.120
So, we started as a residential
program and subsequently,

00:00:32.121 --> 00:00:33.635
based on our experiences there,

00:00:33.636 --> 00:00:35.730
we moved on to starting
our own school.

00:00:35.731 --> 00:00:37.210
Here, as a school,

00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:38.860
the model is an integrated model.

00:00:38.861 --> 00:00:43.460
We have children from all
different socioeconomic status.

00:00:43.461 --> 00:00:46.110
Apart from these children who
stay with us in our hostel,

00:00:46.111 --> 00:00:48.380
we have children from
the neighborhood,

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:50.620
again, from a similar background
who come to the school.

00:00:50.621 --> 00:00:53.030
We also have a portion

00:00:53.031 --> 00:00:55.355
of the children who come in,
our fee-paying children.

00:00:55.356 --> 00:00:58.870
The model was designed in

00:00:58.871 --> 00:01:00.990
such a way so that we can ensure

00:01:00.991 --> 00:01:01.540
there is certain amount of

00:01:01.541 --> 00:01:03.010
quality that will be maintained.

00:01:03.011 --> 00:01:04.130
We believe that if you have

00:01:04.131 --> 00:01:05.930
a little bit of
the market facing,

00:01:05.931 --> 00:01:07.670
they will bring in those forces

00:01:07.671 --> 00:01:09.420
to ensure the quality
is retained,

00:01:09.421 --> 00:01:12.040
and also, it is
a sustainable model.

00:01:12.041 --> 00:01:15.570
So, today, I'm here to speak
about The Samhita Academy.

00:01:15.571 --> 00:01:16.795
That is the name of our school.

00:01:16.796 --> 00:01:18.730
We have other projects
which we've started,

00:01:18.731 --> 00:01:20.590
which is like a Platform
for School Leadership

00:01:20.591 --> 00:01:22.930
last year and Skilling
Institute this year.

00:01:22.931 --> 00:01:26.110
These are still in
the very early stages.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.504
So, just to give
a little background

00:01:30.505 --> 00:01:32.950
of the children who are coming

00:01:32.951 --> 00:01:37.825
from the disadvantaged
background,

00:01:37.826 --> 00:01:39.925
this is probably something
you're not familiar with.

00:01:39.926 --> 00:01:41.410
They have lot of what we call the

00:01:41.411 --> 00:01:42.940
hidden baggage that they
come to school with.

00:01:42.941 --> 00:01:46.780
So, apart from the academic
struggles that they have,

00:01:46.781 --> 00:01:49.720
this is something
that they deal with,

00:01:49.721 --> 00:01:52.030
and this is the kind
of diversity,

00:01:52.031 --> 00:01:53.350
and this is the outcomes that we

00:01:53.351 --> 00:01:55.070
can see in the classrooms.

00:01:55.071 --> 00:01:57.510
The blue is the
privileged classes,

00:01:57.511 --> 00:02:00.885
coming with English
exposure, all of that.

00:02:00.886 --> 00:02:03.490
Then, the next lot of children
are the children where we

00:02:03.491 --> 00:02:06.580
support them in
our own hostel facility.

00:02:06.581 --> 00:02:09.815
The third group, which they've
reached grade four now,

00:02:09.816 --> 00:02:11.300
they are the children
who don't get

00:02:11.301 --> 00:02:14.080
any kind of support
outside of school hours.

00:02:14.081 --> 00:02:17.450
Most of our effort
goes in bringing up

00:02:17.451 --> 00:02:19.400
this huge amount of effort trying

00:02:19.401 --> 00:02:22.800
to bring them to grade level.

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:27.650
So, we have a lot of, of course,

00:02:27.651 --> 00:02:29.800
issues that we could think of,

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:32.435
but we narrowed down
to two areas here.

00:02:32.436 --> 00:02:36.155
One was in the whole area
of assessments.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:38.670
So, today, it's
a very manual process.

00:02:38.671 --> 00:02:40.820
The children hand-write it,

00:02:40.821 --> 00:02:43.850
teachers correct it by
hand, question by question.

00:02:43.851 --> 00:02:46.370
Then, there's one total marks
which is given, and then,

00:02:46.371 --> 00:02:48.890
that gets entered into

00:02:48.891 --> 00:02:51.600
some systems for
reporting, all of that.

00:02:51.601 --> 00:02:54.620
But that doesn't really
help me with anything.

00:02:54.621 --> 00:02:55.860
It doesn't help me to remediate.

00:02:55.861 --> 00:02:57.730
It just tells me how
the whole class is doing.

00:02:57.731 --> 00:03:00.080
There are so many children
at this grade,

00:03:00.081 --> 00:03:01.955
this grade, but by topic level,

00:03:01.956 --> 00:03:04.550
by the strategies that
we may have used,

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:09.060
I have no data to tell me what
worked, what didn't work.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.540
Then, the teachers
spend a lot of time

00:03:11.541 --> 00:03:13.030
just doing all this manual work,

00:03:13.031 --> 00:03:15.160
which they could have spent
probably in remediation,

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:18.380
where we find very little time
actually for them to do.

00:03:18.381 --> 00:03:20.630
So, these are some
of the problems.

00:03:20.631 --> 00:03:22.250
We have totaling
errors, analysis.

00:03:22.251 --> 00:03:26.575
Again, here, I'm not just
talking about quantitative,

00:03:26.576 --> 00:03:28.130
how many got it right, wrong,

00:03:28.131 --> 00:03:29.780
but also maybe looking at

00:03:29.781 --> 00:03:31.490
some of the open-ended questions,

00:03:31.491 --> 00:03:34.255
that kind of data,

00:03:34.256 --> 00:03:39.230
and some qualitative input

00:03:39.231 --> 00:03:43.080
on the answers that
are coming out,

00:03:43.081 --> 00:03:45.170
and whether there's understanding

00:03:45.171 --> 00:03:47.300
based on the taxonomy
of the question,

00:03:47.301 --> 00:03:50.205
whether it is a higher [inaudible]
question, is it rote learning.

00:03:50.206 --> 00:03:52.340
So, those data, it's all lost.

00:03:52.341 --> 00:03:54.605
There's nothing there
for us to work on.

00:03:54.606 --> 00:03:59.480
So, that was one of the areas
that we had identified.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:05.330
We don't really have too
much of data right now.

00:04:05.331 --> 00:04:08.020
It's like I said, at
the whole paper level,

00:04:08.021 --> 00:04:09.420
I have some marks.

00:04:09.421 --> 00:04:11.595
This is what the paper
would look like.

00:04:11.596 --> 00:04:12.985
Some of them are like worksheets,

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:16.560
some are foolscap sheets
into which they're writing.

00:04:16.561 --> 00:04:18.875
So, this is where the data is.

00:04:18.876 --> 00:04:24.290
I think, Saurav, you had
suggested some technology.

00:04:24.291 --> 00:04:26.420
&gt;&gt; So, when we work
with them, we realize

00:04:26.421 --> 00:04:30.425
it's a very paper intensive job.

00:04:30.426 --> 00:04:32.930
So, today, in
the corporate world,

00:04:32.931 --> 00:04:34.950
we have intelligent
scanners which scan

00:04:34.951 --> 00:04:37.390
not only [inaudible] data but

00:04:37.391 --> 00:04:39.750
also OCRs have been able

00:04:39.751 --> 00:04:43.450
to match handwriting and
patterns, and things like that.

00:04:43.451 --> 00:04:46.665
So, if a scanner with
an intelligent AI algorithm

00:04:46.666 --> 00:04:50.030
can be put in place which
scans the answer sheet and

00:04:50.031 --> 00:04:51.740
gives some bit of analysis

00:04:51.741 --> 00:04:54.010
almost in a real-time basis for

00:04:54.011 --> 00:04:56.480
the teacher to
undertake so that she

00:04:56.481 --> 00:04:59.810
can then appropriately
take remedial action.

00:04:59.811 --> 00:05:02.195
Today, what is happening is,
there is a teacher bias.

00:05:02.196 --> 00:05:04.595
There is fatigue in
teachers, let's be honest,

00:05:04.596 --> 00:05:08.060
because each teacher
actually translates to,

00:05:08.061 --> 00:05:09.365
what, 30 or 40 children?

00:05:09.366 --> 00:05:10.940
&gt;&gt; Thirty, but she does

00:05:10.941 --> 00:05:13.640
at least 90 children,
covers 90 children.

00:05:13.641 --> 00:05:15.970
&gt;&gt; Ninety children. Even
in the corporate world,

00:05:15.971 --> 00:05:17.875
if you do a span of control,

00:05:17.876 --> 00:05:20.190
we say that's not
a very optimized model.

00:05:20.191 --> 00:05:23.810
So, thinking about
technology implementation,

00:05:23.811 --> 00:05:26.100
more from an intelligence
perspective,

00:05:26.101 --> 00:05:27.410
scanners are available, yes,

00:05:27.411 --> 00:05:29.630
but maybe an intelligent
algorithm which

00:05:29.631 --> 00:05:32.500
captures this information
in a digital format,

00:05:32.501 --> 00:05:35.370
provides real-time analytics,
would be of great help.

00:05:35.371 --> 00:05:37.715
So, you want to add,
Asha, more on this?

00:05:37.716 --> 00:05:39.620
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, thing is,

00:05:39.621 --> 00:05:40.640
this is one of the areas.

00:05:40.641 --> 00:05:41.960
So, what is the kind of

00:05:41.961 --> 00:05:44.420
qualitative analysis
it can give me?

00:05:44.421 --> 00:05:46.160
I don't know because that would

00:05:46.161 --> 00:05:48.915
depend on what is the state
of the technology.

00:05:48.916 --> 00:05:54.240
&gt;&gt; So, you have
the GI or GMAT exam.

00:05:54.241 --> 00:05:55.830
So, it's MCQ, math,

00:05:55.831 --> 00:05:57.880
and quan, and there's
an essay section.

00:05:57.881 --> 00:05:59.180
So, the essay section, there's

00:05:59.181 --> 00:06:00.965
a human grader and
a computer grader.

00:06:00.966 --> 00:06:03.450
So, essentially,
the computer grader also follows

00:06:03.451 --> 00:06:04.940
similar AI algorithms we had

00:06:04.941 --> 00:06:06.965
for each prompt
what we are given.

00:06:06.966 --> 00:06:09.665
Let's say you're talking
about road traffic accidents.

00:06:09.666 --> 00:06:12.065
Your answer should
have certain keywords

00:06:12.066 --> 00:06:13.550
on which you'll be graded.

00:06:13.551 --> 00:06:16.660
So, similarly, when you ask
questions on your subjects,

00:06:16.661 --> 00:06:18.745
maybe science, geography,
history, whatever,

00:06:18.746 --> 00:06:21.660
that particular answer would
have certain keywords or

00:06:21.661 --> 00:06:24.775
certain facts which
a good answer must have.

00:06:24.776 --> 00:06:27.050
So, probably that can be
fed into the algorithm,

00:06:27.051 --> 00:06:28.640
and on those basis,

00:06:28.641 --> 00:06:30.565
a good grade can be given.

00:06:30.566 --> 00:06:32.019
If there's a discrepancy,

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:35.345
a human teacher can
rectify or add to it.

00:06:35.346 --> 00:06:39.085
So, that could be a method
which you could adopt.

00:06:39.086 --> 00:06:41.870
&gt;&gt; Right, Richa.
You had a question.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:49.180
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]. Actually, when
I was visiting MIT last time,

00:06:49.181 --> 00:06:51.065
some of the people there told me

00:06:51.066 --> 00:06:55.970
how they [inaudible]
really large courses

00:06:55.971 --> 00:06:57.715
with several hundred students.

00:06:57.716 --> 00:06:59.820
I think there's technology
available, for example,

00:06:59.821 --> 00:07:02.860
in the US to scan a bunch
of answers [inaudible] ,

00:07:02.861 --> 00:07:05.820
automatically split them
in different questions.

00:07:05.821 --> 00:07:08.029
So, each teaching
assistant specializes

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:09.860
in grading a few questions.

00:07:09.861 --> 00:07:12.620
So, even if that
grading is manual,

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:15.690
the intelligence doesn't have
to be just in evaluation.

00:07:15.691 --> 00:07:18.560
The intelligence can also
be in routing and matching

00:07:18.561 --> 00:07:21.950
the answers to the right person

00:07:21.951 --> 00:07:27.040
[inaudible] who's human skill
more efficiently.

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:29.880
&gt;&gt; [inaudible]. Just
go into the next area,

00:07:29.881 --> 00:07:31.590
which is likely more specialized,

00:07:31.591 --> 00:07:35.850
which is from our special
education department. Yes.

00:07:35.851 --> 00:07:37.260
&gt;&gt; Also, I've a question about

00:07:37.261 --> 00:07:38.550
the paradigm of
scanning [inaudible].

00:07:38.551 --> 00:07:41.820
So, you need to do it one at
a time or you just [inaudible] ,

00:07:41.821 --> 00:07:44.490
because lots of AI involved

00:07:44.491 --> 00:07:48.255
[inaudible] batch build
unsupervised models.

00:07:48.256 --> 00:07:50.190
For example, models of what

00:07:50.191 --> 00:07:53.400
character sequences
will occur [inaudible].

00:07:53.401 --> 00:07:56.160
That could be very well used

00:07:56.161 --> 00:07:58.350
to improve the accuracy of

00:07:58.351 --> 00:08:01.365
batch [inaudible] than
scan one at a time.

00:08:01.366 --> 00:08:03.580
&gt;&gt; That's agreed
upon. So, today you

00:08:03.581 --> 00:08:05.095
see they're in a manual stage.

00:08:05.096 --> 00:08:07.600
So, whatever technology
solutions that we get out

00:08:07.601 --> 00:08:10.575
of this session and
this two days of workshop,

00:08:10.576 --> 00:08:13.010
and we do a technical
feasibility and

00:08:13.011 --> 00:08:15.830
a financial viability
for the NGO to adopt.

00:08:15.831 --> 00:08:18.030
They would be more
than happy to get it.

00:08:18.031 --> 00:08:20.810
They're here to seek
help more to move from

00:08:20.811 --> 00:08:25.225
that manual to intelligent
digital journey. Yeah.

00:08:25.226 --> 00:08:30.330
&gt;&gt; We are actually
[inaudible] in one area.

00:08:30.331 --> 00:08:36.450
So we are building

00:08:36.451 --> 00:08:39.075
something similar but we
come from a different model.

00:08:39.076 --> 00:08:40.485
So we are integrating,

00:08:40.486 --> 00:08:42.435
so we came from a smartphone,

00:08:42.436 --> 00:08:44.310
doing exams on smartphone models,

00:08:44.311 --> 00:08:45.450
but I don't think that works.

00:08:45.451 --> 00:08:47.280
But as part of this,

00:08:47.281 --> 00:08:49.350
since we had the
entire infrastructure

00:08:49.351 --> 00:08:50.970
built around evaluation.

00:08:50.971 --> 00:08:54.450
We are also building
the scanning mode into it.

00:08:54.451 --> 00:08:56.355
Which is basically,
you just a bunch,

00:08:56.356 --> 00:08:58.800
there are scanners which
you can bulk feed it kind

00:08:58.801 --> 00:09:00.930
of pulls it from

00:09:00.931 --> 00:09:04.220
that and segregates into
the questions and stuff.

00:09:04.221 --> 00:09:06.525
What we also are working

00:09:06.526 --> 00:09:08.790
together with this is
also a question back

00:09:08.791 --> 00:09:10.830
that goes hand-in-hand
with it there where you

00:09:10.831 --> 00:09:11.970
talk about Bloom stack and so

00:09:11.971 --> 00:09:13.440
on, whatever it is you want to.

00:09:13.441 --> 00:09:15.660
So you can actually
set questions based

00:09:15.661 --> 00:09:18.450
on some learning models,

00:09:18.451 --> 00:09:20.940
and than you evaluate it.

00:09:20.941 --> 00:09:23.460
Since I'm a teacher myself,

00:09:23.461 --> 00:09:26.370
this thing about
albeit taking AI,

00:09:26.371 --> 00:09:27.765
taking the role of a teachers,

00:09:27.766 --> 00:09:29.070
I'm still not comfortable,

00:09:29.071 --> 00:09:31.230
because you just kill creativity.

00:09:31.231 --> 00:09:33.555
Someone doesn't answer
the way whatever.

00:09:33.556 --> 00:09:35.580
I mean, I don't
like that anymore,

00:09:35.581 --> 00:09:40.200
but nonetheless, certain
other aspects can be done.

00:09:40.201 --> 00:09:42.495
We are doing some work, I'll
talk to you after that.

00:09:42.496 --> 00:09:44.480
&gt;&gt; That's great idea.
That's a good dialog

00:09:44.481 --> 00:09:45.960
and a good discussion to have.

00:09:45.961 --> 00:09:47.620
So let's just move, keep
moving on the next.

00:09:47.621 --> 00:09:49.510
&gt;&gt; Yes. The next areas in

00:09:49.511 --> 00:09:52.550
our earlier reading program
and I'll get to you.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:57.750
&gt;&gt; One of our
important observation,

00:09:57.751 --> 00:09:59.510
learning has been and

00:09:59.511 --> 00:10:02.740
that the head-start that

00:10:02.741 --> 00:10:05.945
we can give our kids
is learning to read.

00:10:05.946 --> 00:10:08.525
Because most of them
enter grade one,

00:10:08.526 --> 00:10:11.730
don't have skills in terms of

00:10:11.731 --> 00:10:13.740
literacy competency let alone

00:10:13.741 --> 00:10:15.990
reading in English
and when they start,

00:10:15.991 --> 00:10:19.520
when they need to access
content in English,

00:10:19.521 --> 00:10:22.080
then it becomes like
a double barrier for them.

00:10:22.081 --> 00:10:25.370
So all our grade one
and grade two kids

00:10:25.371 --> 00:10:27.615
go through something
called a reading program.

00:10:27.616 --> 00:10:32.030
Now for that, we have to spend
a lot of time doing like,

00:10:32.031 --> 00:10:34.610
whole-class screening
to understand

00:10:34.611 --> 00:10:37.090
who our kids who are at risk,

00:10:37.091 --> 00:10:38.785
who are kids who
are at high risk,

00:10:38.786 --> 00:10:40.920
who are kids who are
already proficient,

00:10:40.921 --> 00:10:42.560
and how can we accelerate

00:10:42.561 --> 00:10:45.055
the kids who are
already proficient.

00:10:45.056 --> 00:10:47.640
Then once we do get them into

00:10:47.641 --> 00:10:50.520
groups where we do
systematic intervention,

00:10:50.521 --> 00:10:53.070
it is the progress monitoring,

00:10:53.071 --> 00:10:55.065
we do know we've followed

00:10:55.066 --> 00:10:56.805
three things in
our reading program.

00:10:56.806 --> 00:10:58.360
Read to Self, Read to Someone,

00:10:58.361 --> 00:11:00.520
Listen to Reading and
then the teachers are

00:11:00.521 --> 00:11:03.000
one who is kind of a facilitator,

00:11:03.001 --> 00:11:07.000
who kind of makes
the whole reading process,

00:11:07.001 --> 00:11:10.725
she just demonstrates
the whole thing.

00:11:10.726 --> 00:11:14.755
So when we do know
that time spent on

00:11:14.756 --> 00:11:16.920
reading by yourself is so

00:11:16.921 --> 00:11:20.445
important and that leads to
a high amount of success,

00:11:20.446 --> 00:11:22.000
we are unable to monitor

00:11:22.001 --> 00:11:24.310
that independent reading program.

00:11:24.311 --> 00:11:26.470
We're talking about
six years old and

00:11:26.471 --> 00:11:27.760
seven-year-old who do not

00:11:27.761 --> 00:11:29.920
have much self-regulation, right?

00:11:29.921 --> 00:11:33.250
So a lot of things get losses

00:11:33.251 --> 00:11:36.830
to how are they reading
during that independent time.

00:11:36.831 --> 00:11:39.030
A teacher will maximum have,

00:11:39.031 --> 00:11:41.195
say about 15 minutes a week,

00:11:41.196 --> 00:11:43.455
to listen to one child
read, right?

00:11:43.456 --> 00:11:44.755
But the rest of the time they're

00:11:44.756 --> 00:11:47.415
reading to someone or
reading to a peer,

00:11:47.416 --> 00:11:51.990
and those are
very vital opportunities

00:11:51.991 --> 00:11:53.565
that give us a lot of

00:11:53.566 --> 00:11:57.840
deep skill building
that happens in

00:11:57.841 --> 00:12:00.030
children and that
is also something

